.\" cgra.5 - the .cgra configuration file format
.TH CGRA 5 "2026" "cgra 0.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
cgra \- configuration file format for the CGRA accelerator front end
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
~/.config/cgra/config.cgra
~/.config/cgra/*.cgra , ~/.config/cgra/conf.d/*.cgra
/etc/cgra/*.cgra
./.cgra/*.cgra
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B .cgra
file describes, declaratively, how
.BR cgra (1)
should drive the fabric: named connection
.I devices,
array
.I modes,
multi-stage
.I pipelines,
and output
.I "io profiles."
The syntax is INI-flavoured: a sequence of named sections, each holding
.BI key " = " value
directives.
.PP
A
.B #
begins a trailing comment; a line whose first non-blank character is
.B ;
is a whole-line comment. Blank lines are ignored. Every entity is merged
.B by name
across all loaded files (see
.BR "FILE DISCOVERY" ),
so a later file overrides individual fields of an entity defined earlier rather
than replacing it wholesale.
.PP
Syntax errors in any discovered file stop loading and report the source.
A failed file, including its includes, does not apply partial overrides.
Unknown PE fields are errors.
.SH SECTIONS
A section header is
.BI [ type " " name ].
Four section types exist.
.SS [device NAME]
A connection profile.
.TP
.B port
Serial device path (e.g.\&
.IR /dev/ttyUSB1 )
or
.B sim:
for the in-process emulator.
.TP
.B baud
Baud rate (default 115200).
.TP
.B timeout
Per-reply timeout in milliseconds.
.TP
.B board
Informational board tag
.RB ( basys3 ", " nexys_a7 ).
.SS [mode NAME]
An array configuration. A mode either selects a
.I pattern
that generates the device's R x C PE words, or places PEs by hand.
.TP
.B doc
One-line description, shown by
.BR "cgra modes" .
.TP
.B pattern
One of
.BR diagonal " (default), " reduce ", " custom ", " systolic ", " conv ", " scan .
See
.BR PATTERNS .
.TP
.B op
ALU opcode for
.B diagonal
and
.B reduce
modes. See
.BR OPCODES .
.TP
.BR a ", " b
Operand sources. See
.BR "OPERAND SOURCES" .
Defaults:
.B a
=
.BR north ,
.B b
=
.BR west .
.TP
.B steps
.BR rows " (device row count), " auto ", or an integer in 0..255."
Zero means no array steps; out-of-range counts are rejected.
.TP
.B out
Output tap:
.B diag
(the min(rows, cols) diagonal PEs) or
.BI "pe " R , C
(a single PE).
.TP
.B reset
.BR once " (default) or " each .
Clear PE registers at the start of each nonempty run; each also clears
them before every subsequent input chunk. See RESET AND STATE below.
.TP
.BI "pe " R , C " = op=" \fIO\fB " a=" \fISA\fB " b=" \fISB
For
.BR "pattern = custom" ,
place one PE (coordinates must fit the selected geometry). Unlisted PEs are
.BR nop .
.SS [pipeline NAME]
A chain of modes; the host feeds each stage's output into the next.
.TP
.B doc
Description.
.TP
.BI "stage = " MODE " [imm=" K ]
One stage. Repeat for each. The first stage may be binary (consume the
.I a
and
.I b
inputs); later stages are unary.
.B imm=K
sets that stage's immediate.
.SS [io NAME]
An output data-binding profile.
.TP
.B format
.BR dec ", " hex ", " bin " or " json .
.TP
.B width
Datapath width; must be 16 if supplied.
.TP
.B sep
.BR ws ", " comma " or " newline .
.SH PATTERNS
How a mode maps onto the device mesh (default 4x4). The single register per PE and edge-only
operand injection (north per column, west per row) constrain every pattern.
.TP
.B diagonal
Element-wise binary op:
.I a
enters from the north,
.I b
from the west, off-diagonal PEs are PASS chains, and each diagonal
PE computes one result. min(rows,cols) lanes per transaction.
.TP
.B reduce
MAC/ACC accumulation into PE(0,0); one element pair per step, giving a scalar
(e.g.\& a dot product).
.TP
.B custom
Hand-placed PEs via
.BI "pe " R , C
directives; unlisted PEs are NOP. Output taps default to the diagonal or a
single
.BI "pe " R , C
via
.BR out .
.TP
.B systolic
Weight-stationary matrix-vector y = A*x, driven by
.BR "cgra matvec" .
The vector is held in the PE immediates; the matrix streams in one row per
step; the array is tiled over rows x cols blocks. Not a plain vector
.BR run .
.TP
.B conv
1-D convolution as a Toeplitz matrix-vector, driven by
.BR "cgra conv" .
.TP
.B scan
Inclusive prefix scan on row 0, driven by
.BR "cgra scan" .
The block's elements arrive on the north edge rather than sitting in the PE
immediates, so the configuration does not depend on the data and is loaded once
for the whole vector instead of once per block.
.SH PATTERN FIELDS
All patterns accept doc and pattern. The additional accepted fields are:
.TP
.B diagonal
op, a, b, steps, out=diag, reset.
.TP
.B custom
pe R,C, steps, out=diag or out=pe R,C, reset.
Top-level op, a and b are rejected; define them on individual PE lines.
.TP
.B reduce
op, a, b, steps, and optionally reset=once. The result is always PE(0,0).
Explicit out or reset=each is rejected.
.TP
.BR systolic ", " conv ", " scan
No additional fields. The dedicated matvec, conv and scan commands choose
parameters from their command-line arguments, independently of these entries.
.PP
Inapplicable fields fail compilation before configuration or execution.
Fields merge across files, so changing pattern does not discard earlier
definitions. An empty assignment (e.g. op =) clears an ordinary field.
Use a new mode name when an inherited custom PE layout must be discarded.
.SH RESET AND STATE
For diagonal and custom modes, reset=once starts each nonempty run with zero
registers and preserves state between chunks of that run. Separate run
commands or mode_run calls start afresh. reset=each clears before every chunk.
An empty diagonal/custom input configures the mesh without resetting or
stepping it. A reduction resets once per run, including an empty input
(which returns zero), and retains its accumulator throughout the run.
.PP
These rules apply to v2 and v3. A stateless v3 diagonal may reset later chunks
when enough steps settle every diagonal result, enabling NACK retries without
changing the output. With fewer than min(rows,cols) steps, routing registers
can carry data between chunks: once and each can give different outputs even
for an ordinary arithmetic opcode.
.SH OPERAND SOURCES
.PP
Input vector A feeds north and vector B feeds west independently of the
ALU operand positions. For example, a=const(0), b=north computes negation
with op=sub; a=west, b=north computes B-A with op=sub.
A live west-edge source requires vector B. South/east sources require custom
placement. Each PE has one shared immediate: two distinct constants are rejected.
Immediates must fit a 16-bit signed value or unsigned bit pattern
(-32768..65535); an override replaces both constant operands on a PE.
Values for the
.BR a / b
directives and the
.BR a= / b=
fields of a
.B pe
line:
.TP
.BR north ", " west ", " self ", " zero
The registered output of that neighbour; on the edges,
.B north
of row 0 and
.B west
of column 0 read the input ports.
.B self
is the PE's own register (feedback);
.B zero
is a constant 0.
.TP
.BI const( K )
A 16-bit signed immediate
.IR K .
Bare
.B const
takes its value from
.B --imm
at the command line.
.SH OPCODES
.B nop pass add sub mul mac and or xor shl shr max min abs acc const
.PP
Arithmetic is 16-bit signed and wraps;
.B mul
and
.B mac
keep the low 16 bits of the product;
.B shr
is arithmetic.
.SH DIRECTIVES
Two directives may appear outside any section.
.TP
.BI "include \(dq" file .cgra \(dq
Parse another file inline. Searched, in order: relative to the including file,
each
.B CGRA_PATH
entry, the user config dir and its
.IR stdlib/ ,
then the configured installed standard library and
.IR /etc/cgra .
The installed path defaults to PREFIX/share/cgra/stdlib, normally
/usr/local/share/cgra/stdlib; pkgdatadir can override its parent at build and
install time. DESTDIR is only a staging directory, excluded from runtime paths.
Installed includes work before init.
.TP
.BI "set " NAME " = " value
Define a variable.
.BI $ NAME
and
.BI ${ NAME }
expand it in any later value.
.SH FILE DISCOVERY
Files are loaded from several roots, lowest to highest precedence:
.IP 1. 4
built-in defaults (compiled in);
.IP 2. 4
.IR /etc/cgra ;
.IP 3. 4
the user directory
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cgra
or
.IR ~/.config/cgra );
.IP 4. 4
each
.B CGRA_PATH
directory (colon-separated, like
.BR PATH );
.IP 5. 4
project-local:
.B CGRA_CONFIG
if set, else
.IR ./.cgra/ ;
.IP 6. 4
the
.B "cgra -c"
file (highest).
.PP
Within each directory,
.I config.cgra
loads first, then the remaining
.I *.cgra
alphabetically, then a
.I conf.d/
drop-in directory alphabetically \(em so
.I conf.d/99_overrides.cgra
beats
.I conf.d/01_base.cgra
by name (the Nginx/systemd convention).
.B "cgra config"
prints the resolved paths.
.SH EXAMPLES
A small, self-contained configuration:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[device lab]
port    = /dev/ttyUSB1
baud    = 115200
board   = basys3

# element-wise: out = max(a, 0)
[mode relu]
doc     = out = max(a, 0)
pattern = diagonal
op      = max
a       = north
b       = const(0)

# hand-placed PEs
[mode madd]
pattern = custom
out     = diag
pe 0,0  = op=mac a=north b=west
pe 1,1  = op=add a=self  b=const(1)

# chain modes: out = a*3 + 7
[pipeline saxpy]
stage   = muli imm=3
stage   = addi imm=7

# reusable gain via a variable
set GAIN = 7
[mode amplify]
pattern = diagonal
op      = mul
a       = north
b       = const($GAIN)

[io hex]
format  = hex
sep     = ws
.EE
.RE
.PP
Validate it with
.BR "cgra check" ,
list what loaded with
.BR "cgra modes" ,
and inspect a compilation with
.BR "cgra show relu -v" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cgra/config.cgra
User configuration, written by
.BR "cgra init" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/cgra/stdlib/
User copy of the standard library, pulled in with
.BR include .
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/cgra/stdlib/
Installed standard library (PREFIX defaults to /usr/local).
.TP
.I /etc/cgra/
System-wide configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cgra (1),
.BR libcgra (3)
.PP
The GNU info manual
.RB ( "info cgra" )
has a tutorial treatment of the same material.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the Advanced Logic Design course, University of Trento.

.SH GEOMETRY
The selected device reports rows and columns through ID. Diagonal modes use
min(rows,cols) lanes, matrix modes use rows x cols tiles, and scans use cols
lanes. Explicit PE coordinates and output taps must fit the target device;
parsing allows indices 0 through 15, with the actual bounds checked during
compilation. Offline check and show without an explicit device use 4x4.

.SH VALIDATION LIMITS
Integer tokens must be complete decimal, hexadecimal or octal literals.
Baud must be positive; timeout must be 0..INT_MAX milliseconds. Zero means
an immediate reply poll. New device profiles default to 115200 baud and a
2000 ms timeout. Invalid formats, separators, coordinates and trailing
section tokens are rejected. Semantic mode validation is also performed by
check and before execution with the selected geometry.
.PP
Variables must be defined before use; malformed or unknown references are
errors. $$ emits a literal dollar sign. Variable names begin with a letter
or underscore and continue with letters, digits or underscores.
Names allow 47 bytes, small fields 31, values and stored paths 191.
Oversized fields and exhausted variable/stage/PE/file capacities cause errors.
CGRA_PATH is processed as a full list. Embedded NUL bytes are rejected.
Parsing a failed file, including its includes, leaves the context unchanged.
